# WardenX — environment template.
# Copy to `.env` for local work: `cp .env.example .env` is SUFFICIENT to bring the local stack up
# with `bun run db:reset && bun run dev`. NEVER commit real secrets; `.env` is git-ignored.
#
# Every value below is a LOCAL DEV PLACEHOLDER and matches docker-compose.yml, which is the single
# local provisioning path (F-016 / #43). Until then this file named a role — `wardenx` — that no
# provisioning path ever created, on a port that matched neither path, and omitted two variables the
# migration runner requires, so following its own "copy to .env" instruction could not start the
# stack. The onboarding runbook is docs/START_PROJECT.md.

# --- Core datastore (multi-tenant PostgreSQL) ---
# The compose Postgres (docker-compose.yml `postgres`, published on 5432). Two URLs, deliberately:
#   · DATABASE_ADMIN_URL — the superuser. `bun run db:migrate` needs owner-level DDL, and it is what
#     CREATES the four wardenx_* roles, so it cannot itself be one of them.
#   · DATABASE_URL       — the NOBYPASSRLS application role the API and worker actually run as.
#     It does not exist until `db:migrate` has run once.
# WARDENX_APP_PASSWORD is the password `db:migrate` sets on wardenx_app; it must match DATABASE_URL and
# the compose stack's value, or a host-side migrate silently breaks the Docker API's sign-in.
DATABASE_ADMIN_URL=postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/wardenx
DATABASE_URL=postgres://wardenx_app:wardenx-app-dev-password@localhost:5432/wardenx
DATABASE_PLATFORM_URL=postgres://wardenx_platform_login:wardenx-platform-dev-password@localhost:5432/wardenx
WARDENX_APP_PASSWORD=wardenx-app-dev-password
WARDENX_TEST_DATABASE_URL=

# --- Cache / queue (Valkey/Redis for BullMQ) ---
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# --- Medicare launch gateways (X12 generation/parsing + NGS J6 transport) ---
# Bearer tokens are service credentials. Inject production values from a secret manager.
# Production default is enabled. Set to `false` only to deliberately disable claim submission and
# MAC acknowledgement workers; their routes then fail closed with 503 instead of accepting work.
M23_LAUNCH_GATEWAY_ENABLED=true
M23_X12_GATEWAY_URL=https://x12-gateway.example.invalid
M23_X12_GATEWAY_TOKEN=replace-with-a-32-character-service-token
M23_MAC_GATEWAY_URL=https://mac-gateway.example.invalid
M23_MAC_GATEWAY_TOKEN=replace-with-a-32-character-service-token
M23_GATEWAY_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# --- MinIO access-log webhook (M22 download confirmation) ---
# The bearer MinIO's audit webhook presents to POST /api/v1/webhooks/minio/access-log. Unset => the endpoint answers
# 503 and records nothing. In production deploy.sh generates it and compose wires it into both minio and the api.
MINIO_ACCESS_LOG_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=replace-with-a-32-character-service-token

# --- Medicaid (M24) Oregon launch gateway capability gate ---
# Production default is enabled (unset = on); set `false` only to deliberately disable the Medicaid submit and
# poll workers. Outside production an unset value leaves it OFF so nothing calls out by accident. This is a
# capability gate only: endpoints and secrets come from each provider entity's trading-partner credential
# (and the workers additionally need M24_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY).
M24_LAUNCH_GATEWAY_ENABLED=true
M24_GATEWAY_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# --- Tenant provisioning gateway (KMS wrap, tenant storage policy, invitation delivery) ---
# NOT PROVIDED BY THIS REPO. This addresses an EXTERNAL service you must supply and deploy: five
# endpoints backing per-tenant DEK wrap/unwrap, tenant storage provisioning + destruction, and
# invitation delivery. `compose.yml`'s `provision` service is a different thing (a one-shot DB role
# provisioner). The contract is in deploy/production/PROVISION_GATEWAY.md; ADR-0038 §3 is why it is
# external. The `.example.invalid` hosts below are RFC 2606 placeholders and are REJECTED by the
# production env validator — replace them or the boot fails closed (#115).
# Production default is enabled. Set to `false` only to disable tenant provisioning, key rotation,
# storage destruction, and first-admin invitation delivery. It does NOT disable tenant encryption:
# M34_TENANT_KEK remains required for existing encrypted data.
M34_PROVISION_GATEWAY_ENABLED=true
M34_PROVISION_GATEWAY_URL=https://provision-gateway.example.invalid
M34_PROVISION_GATEWAY_TOKEN=replace-with-a-32-character-service-token
M34_INVITATION_ACCEPT_URL=https://app.example.invalid/tenant-invitations
# M28 FHIR gateway (F-035 / #95) — the ABSOLUTE URL of this deployment's `/fhir/oauth2/token`. It is the
# `aud` a SMART Backend Services client assertion must carry, and audience binding is what stops an
# assertion minted for a partner's other FHIR server being replayed against this one. Production default
# is enabled. Set FHIR_TOKEN_ENDPOINT_ENABLED=false to disable this assertion-auth flow; it fails closed
# with 503 while other FHIR token authentication remains available.
FHIR_TOKEN_ENDPOINT_ENABLED=true
FHIR_TOKEN_ENDPOINT_URL=https://fhir.example.invalid/fhir/oauth2/token
# M28 §2.2 — the platform HMAC key that signs the persisted `fhir_capability_snapshot`. The hourly
# `fhir.capability-snapshot-validator` FAILS (DLQ alert) without it, rather than reporting an unverified
# snapshot as valid. Rotating it makes every previously signed snapshot fail verification.
# FHIR_CAPABILITY_SIGNING_KEY=<at least 32 random characters>
M34_PROVISION_GATEWAY_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# --- Transactional email (M36, Postmark; docs/runbooks/transactional-email-postmark.md) ---
# Leave BLANK to keep the provider UNBOUND: production then drops email and the funeral / collaborator / auditor
# invite routes answer 503 LINK_DELIVERY_UNAVAILABLE (fail closed). Set ALL THREE to bind it; a partial set refuses
# to boot. The server token is a secret - never commit it. FROM must be a Postmark-verified sender signature/domain
# (SPF + DKIM + Return-Path), and POSTMARK_SENDER_VERIFIED=true is your attestation that it is.
POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN=
POSTMARK_FROM_ADDRESS=
POSTMARK_SENDER_VERIFIED=
# Optional: Postmark message stream (default "outbound"). POSTMARK_API_BASE_URL is for tests only (default https://api.postmarkapp.com).
# POSTMARK_MESSAGE_STREAM=outbound
# The platform KEK that wraps every tenant's DEK (#105 / ADR-0045). `<kekId>:<base64 32 bytes>`.
# Generate: printf 'kek-1:%s\n' "$(openssl rand -base64 32)"
# Rotating? Move the OLD entry into M34_TENANT_KEK_RETIRED (comma-separated) or every key ring
# still naming it becomes unreadable — a retired KEK is unwrap-only, never used for new wraps.
M34_TENANT_KEK=kek-1:replace-with-openssl-rand-base64-32
M34_TENANT_KEK_RETIRED=

# F-230 (#248) — per-tenant DoseSpot WEBHOOK secrets, a JSON map `{"<organizationId>":"<secret, >=16 chars>"}`.
# UNSET (the default — no DoseSpot credentials exist for any tenant) ⇒ `POST /webhooks/dosespot` is DISABLED and
# answers 503 to everything; there is no default, shared or derived secret. The signature scheme (HMAC-SHA256 of the
# raw body, `X-DoseSpot-Signature` / `X-DoseSpot-Timestamp`) is M12 spec §7.4 and has NOT been verified against a
# real DoseSpot delivery. A tenant also needs a provisioned M34 key ring (receipts are DEK-encrypted at rest).
DOSESPOT_WEBHOOK_SECRETS=

# GAP-037 — per-tenant DoseSpot ADAPTER configuration, a JSON map
#   {"<organizationId>":{"baseUrl":"https://my.staging.dosespot.com/webapi/v2","subscriptionKey":"…",
#    "clinicId":"…","clinicKey":"…","serviceClinicianId":"…","epcsCertified":false}}
# UNSET / malformed / an incomplete or non-https entry ⇒ the adapter is UNBOUND for that tenant: every DoseSpot call
# rejects (non-retryable), nothing is queued for transmission, and signed orders keep activating locally. There is
# no default, shared or derived credential. Provide it from the secrets manager; never commit a value. The endpoints
# and field names are NOT sandbox-verified (docs/history/m12-dosespot-adapter.md) — start with the staging URL.
DOSESPOT_TENANT_CONFIGS=

# F-172 (#248) — the platform KEK that wraps M35's platform-singleton `patient_pseudonym_key` (§D6/D15).
# A SEPARATE secret from M34_TENANT_KEK — never reuse the same value. `<kekId>:<base64 32 bytes>`.
# Generate: printf 'kek-1:%s\n' "$(openssl rand -base64 32)"
M35_PSEUDONYM_KEY_KEK=kek-1:replace-with-openssl-rand-base64-32
M35_PSEUDONYM_KEY_KEK_RETIRED=

# --- Object storage (MinIO / S3-compatible, per-tenant buckets) ---
# Var names must match s3ObjectStoreConfigFromEnv (apps/backend/src/modules/22-documents/seams/
# s3-object-store.ts) exactly — S3_* here was stale/never-read (TASK-040F fix); MINIO_* is real.
# Absent, M22 binds a refusing deny-stub instead of a live object store (documented, fail-closed).
MINIO_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
# Optional external origin for browser-facing presigned URLs; defaults to MINIO_ENDPOINT.
MINIO_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
MINIO_ACCESS_KEY=wardenx
MINIO_SECRET_KEY=wardenx
MINIO_REGION=us-east-1

# --- Auth (Better Auth, ADR-0026) ---
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=replace-me-in-real-deployments
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:5173

# --- Runtime ---
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- SD-055 rate-limit tier override (WX-PR-018; optional) ---
# Unset in production — DEFAULT_RATE_LIMIT_TIERS (30/min unauthenticated, 600/min authenticated)
# applies. A serial Playwright E2E run signs in many times from one machine, all sharing the same
# `ip:<loopback>` unauthenticated-tier bucket (the sign-in call is inherently pre-auth, so it has
# nowhere else to bucket) — raise this for local dev / E2E only, never in a real deployment.
#
# The AUTHENTICATED tier needs raising for the same run: one full serial E2E pass issues ~805 requests
# in ~43s (measured, 33 specs) — ~1113/min against the 600/min default, in one `ip:` bucket. Its failure
# mode is deceptive: the patients census 429s, the chart fixture polls an empty table, and the run goes
# red in whichever spec came next, looking like missing seed data rather than a rate limit.
# RATE_LIMIT_UNAUTHENTICATED_PER_MINUTE=300
# RATE_LIMIT_AUTHENTICATED_PER_MINUTE=3000

# ── HOPE iQIES submission header: WardenX as the software vendor (CMS SFTWR_* control items) ───
# Required to build HOPE iQIES packages; packaging refuses with a 422 naming any missing key.
# The product version comes from the image's APP_VERSION / GIT_SHA. Each provider entity's iQIES
# facility ID (FAC_ID) is provider_entity.iqies_facility_id, set at provisioning.
# WARDENX_SOFTWARE_VENDOR_ID=123456789          # vendor federal EIN, 9 digits
# WARDENX_SOFTWARE_VENDOR_NAME=                 # ≤ 30 characters
# WARDENX_SOFTWARE_VENDOR_EMAIL=                # ≤ 50 characters
# WARDENX_SOFTWARE_PRODUCT_NAME=WardenX         # optional, default WardenX

# GAP-096 — set to `true` ONLY on a single-tenant deployment: it lets a tenant `clinical_safety_lead` act as
# M32 AI Safety Owner. Unset = multi-tenant (the safe default); any value other than true/false refuses boot.
# WARDENX_SINGLE_TENANT_DEPLOYMENT=false

# GAP-055 / ADR-0035 — M18 EVV: require a valid device attestation AND bind each capture (check-in, check-out,
# offline-sync) to the session's attested device. Leave unset/false until an attested mobile client exists:
# enforcing it with no such client makes every production capture return 403. Unset/false = capture records the
# clinician's most-recently-approved device (a client-supplied evvDeviceId must still equal it); the
# assigned-clinician and visit-window guards apply either way. Any value other than true/false refuses boot.
# EVV_DEVICE_ATTESTATION_ENFORCED=false

# GAP-009 — closed registration: `POST /api/auth/sign-up/email` needs an `x-invite-token` header naming a
# pending invitation for the same email (the web invite page sends it), and magic-link sign-in may not
# create accounts. Unset = STRICT in production, OPEN in dev (the seeds sign users up without an invite).
# Set `true` to test strict mode locally, `false` to open it. Any other value refuses boot.
# WARDENX_SIGNUP_REQUIRES_INVITE=false

# --- Medicare HETS 270/271 eligibility (M23 §4.4) ---
# OPTIONAL. Unset M23_HETS_TRANSPORT = eligibility checks stay UNBOUND (POST /billing/eligibility/check
# answers 503; never a fabricated result). `sandbox` answers with synthetic 271 fixtures for local dev and
# is REFUSED when NODE_ENV=production. `core_mime` posts CAQH CORE MIME to CMS HETS (client certificate)
# or to a clearinghouse / Network Service Vendor (bearer token). See WARDENX_INTEGRATIONS §6.4.
# M23_HETS_TRANSPORT=sandbox
# M23_HETS_SANDBOX_SCENARIO=
# M23_HETS_ENDPOINT_URL=https://hets-or-clearinghouse.example.invalid/core
# M23_HETS_SUBMITTER_ID=
# M23_HETS_AUTH_TOKEN=
# M23_HETS_CLIENT_CERT_FILE=
# M23_HETS_CLIENT_KEY_FILE=
# M23_HETS_USAGE=P
# M23_HETS_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# M5 geocode_address (GAP-118). A geocode sends a patient's HOME ADDRESS to a third party, so the geocoder is
# fail-closed: no vendor is bound until ALL THREE are set AND GEOCODER_VENDOR names an adapter registered in
# apps/backend/src/modules/05-patient/geocoder.ts (the registry ships EMPTY — a vendor adapter is code, not config).
# Until then `m5.patient.address_changed` deliveries dead-letter (replayable) and GAP-054's geocode-coverage gauge
# keeps reporting the un-geocoded patients.
# GEOCODER_VENDOR=
# GEOCODER_API_KEY=
# GEOCODER_BAA_ATTESTED=false

# audit.archive (GAP-113, M1 §4.8): monthly cold-tier copy of CLOSED audit_event partitions older than the hot
# window. Needs BOTH a bucket here and the MINIO_* credentials above, or the job logs a skip and does nothing.
# The bucket must already exist and be private; it holds the platform's full audit trail (every tenant), so give it
# encryption at rest and object lock / versioning per your retention class. The job NEVER drops a partition;
# AUDIT_ARCHIVE_DETACH=true additionally detaches each archived (and verified) partition from the hot table —
# leave it off until M4's retention policy for archived ranges is agreed.
# AUDIT_ARCHIVE_BUCKET=
# AUDIT_ARCHIVE_PREFIX=audit-archive
# AUDIT_ARCHIVE_HOT_MONTHS=13
# AUDIT_ARCHIVE_DETACH=false
# AUDIT_ARCHIVE_ROWS_PER_PART=20000

# M29 MLLP listener (GAP-086, spec §4.11). OFF unless BOTH are set: run the dedicated process (PROCESS_ROLE=listener, or
# `docker compose --profile hl7-listener up -d hl7-listener` in production) AND set the flag below. A listener binds TCP ports that
# accept PHI from partners (mTLS only; cleartext mllp_plain is refused in production), so there is no implicit enable. The inbound
# parse worker needs no flag: it runs in the worker role and processes whatever the listener stored.
# M29_MLLP_LISTENER_ENABLED=false
# LISTENER_HEALTH_PORT=3002
# M29_MLLP_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
